\section{Numerical Verification of the Energy Statements}
\label{app:energy-numerics}

All checks use the model of \cref{def:esfprm} with random weights in double precision: $T=6$ tokens,
$d=16$, $H=2$ heads of dimension $8$, $K=24$ memories, $\kappa_0=1$, signed head weights
$\sigma=(0.3,-0.3)$, $\beta_k=2$, $\kappa_M=0.5$, $\beta_M=3$, and the geodesic injection; inputs
$x$ are normalized embeddings of random clues. Scripts are included with the submission.
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{\Cref{prop:energy-gradient}.} The closed-form drive \eqref{eq:grad-z}--\eqref{eq:grad-u}
  is compared with $-\Proj_w\nabla E$ from automatic differentiation on $200$ random states for the
  flat model and $100$ for the hidden model. The coupling matrices $J_{ts}$ of the flat model are
  assembled explicitly and compared with $J_{st}^\top$, and $\Proj_z\sum_sJ_{ts}z_s$ with the attention
  part of the drive.
  \item \textbf{\Cref{prop:real-spectrum}.} A stationary point is found by iterating
  \eqref{eq:esfprm-step} with $\eta=0.2$ until $\norm{\grad E}<10^{-14}$ ($765$ iterations). The
  tangent Jacobian of $F_E$ (automatic differentiation in an orthonormal basis of the tangent space)
  is compared with $\Id-\operatorname{Hess}E$, where the Riemannian Hessian is
  $\Proj\nabla^2E\,\Proj-\operatorname{M}_\mu$, and with central finite differences of step $10^{-6}$
  along tangent directions. The Hessian eigenvalues lie in $[0.21,1.90]$. For comparison, a random
  one-layer SFPRM map (scalar eigen learning rates $0.3$, $\lambda=0.5$) is iterated to a fixed point
  (residual $<10^{-30}$) and its tangent Jacobian is analysed in the same way.
  \item \textbf{\Cref{thm:lyapunov}.} $\hat L$ is the largest Riemannian Hessian eigenvalue over $50$
  random states ($2.23$) and over the states and midpoints visited by $20$ descent trajectories of
  $20$ steps ($3.05$). $200$ trajectories of $20$ steps are then run at
  $\eta\in\{0.5,1,1.9,3\}/\hat L$; at the three step sizes inside the window the energy never
  increased and the inequality of (i) held at every step (worst slack $-5.9\times10^{-8}$ at
  $1.9/\hat L$), while at $3/\hat L$ the energy increased in $54\%$ of the steps.
  \item \textbf{\Cref{eq:implicit}.} At a stationary point with Hessian condition number $6.5$ and a
  linear loss $\ell(w)=\ip{\omega}{w}$ with random $\omega$, the adjoint system is solved exactly, by
  conjugate gradient, and by the truncated Neumann series $\eta\sum_{k<K}(\Id-\eta\operatorname{Hess}E)^k$
  with $\eta=1/\lambda_{\max}$; the iteration counts to relative error $10^{-3}$ are $7$ and $40$ and
  to $10^{-6}$ they are $11$ and $81$. The resulting derivative of $\ell(w^\star)$ with respect to
  $\kappa_0$ is compared with central finite differences of step $10^{-5}$ through the re-solved
  stationary point.
  \item \textbf{\Cref{eq:ep}.} The nudged minimizer of $E+\beta\ell$ is found by the same iteration
  and \eqref{eq:ep} is evaluated one-sided and centered for $\beta\in\{10^{-1},10^{-2},10^{-3}\}$;
  the relative errors are $2.5$, $0.34$, $0.034$ (one-sided, $O(\beta)$) and $0.050$, $0.0010$,
  $1.1\times10^{-5}$ (centered, $O(\beta^2)$).
\end{itemize}
